--- log ---
2026-05-23T14:25Z HDMX-coding-agent created — recurring main-lock error during a merge-heavy session in aedist-technical-report

2026-05-23T14:55Z MinhHaDuong closed — autoclosed — PR #211
--- body ---
## Context

`skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` line 117 runs unconditionally:

```bash
gh pr merge "$PR" --squash --delete-branch # harness-extension-point
```

When invoked from inside a git worktree, the **server-side squash succeeds**, but
`gh`'s local `--delete-branch` cleanup then fails:

```
failed to run git: fatal: 'main' is already used by worktree at <primary-repo>
```

`gh pr merge --delete-branch` tries to switch the local checkout off the merged
branch; it can't, because `main` is held by the primary worktree. The command
exits non-zero, so callers (and the `/merge` skill) treat a successful merge as a
failure and start retrying. In a session on 2026-05-23 this bit three consecutive
PRs (#452/#457/#458 in aedist-technical-report) before the operator dropped the flag.

The flag is redundant in every repo that sets `deleteBranchOnMerge: true` (all of
them, per git.md) — GitHub deletes the remote branch on merge regardless.

The script **already** detects worktrees: `in_worktree()` at line ~25, used at
step 4 (line ~121) to skip the base-branch checkout. The merge call simply doesn't
consult it.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Line 117 no longer passes `--delete-branch` when `in_worktree` returns true.
      Outside a worktree, behaviour is unchanged (`--delete-branch` still passed).
- [ ] After the change, a worktree merge prints the merge confirmation and exits 0;
      the remote branch is gone (verify `git ls-remote --heads origin <branch>` empty).
- [ ] First test (source-inspection, no network): assert the merge invocation is
      guarded by `in_worktree`. e.g. a shell/bats check that
      `grep -A2 'gh pr merge' erg-pr-merge` shows the `--delete-branch` variant only
      in the non-worktree branch:

      ```bash
      run grep -c -- '--delete-branch' skills/merge/erg-pr-merge
      [ "$output" -eq 1 ]            # exactly one, in the else/non-worktree path
      run bash -c 'sed -n "/in_worktree;/,/fi/p" skills/merge/erg-pr-merge | grep -c -- "--delete-branch"'
      [ "$output" -eq 0 ]           # never in the worktree path
      ```
- [ ] Comment on line 117 updated to explain the worktree carve-out.

## Guidance

Minimal shape:

```bash
echo "Merging PR #${PR}..."
if in_worktree; then
    # deleteBranchOnMerge cleans the remote; local --delete-branch would fail
    # on the primary worktree's main-lock. See ticket 0170.
    gh pr merge "$PR" --squash # harness-extension-point
else
    gh pr merge "$PR" --squash --delete-branch # harness-extension-point
fi
```

Keep the `# harness-extension-point` marker on whichever line(s) downstream tooling
greps for. Don't refactor the surrounding step-3/step-4 structure.

Sibling from same session: [[0171]] (worktree-path write guard). No dependency.
